== 5.1 Components of Fitness

*Physical fitness* is commonly described through several abilities relevant to health or performance. Health-related components often include cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition. Definitions and classification systems can vary; these components describe different qualities and should not be reduced to a single score.

*Cardiorespiratory endurance* concerns the ability of the heart, lungs and circulation to support sustained activity. *Muscular strength* concerns the force a muscle or group can exert; *muscular endurance* concerns sustaining or repeating effort over time. *Flexibility* concerns the range of movement at a joint. *Body composition* describes proportions of body tissues; it is not a complete measure of an individual's health.

Skill-related components commonly include agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time and speed. *Agility* involves changing direction effectively; *balance* involves maintaining or controlling position; *coordination* involves combining movements effectively. The qualities can overlap in an activity.

#rule-box("Strength or endurance?")[
  A single large force and the ability to repeat or sustain effort describe different qualities. A task may involve both, but the wording identifies which component the question is testing.
]

== 5.2 Principles of Conditioning

*Specificity* means adapting training to the goal and activity. *Progression* means changing demands gradually and appropriately. *Recovery* allows time between demands; *reversibility* describes the loss of some adaptations when training is reduced or stopped. People respond differently, and progress is not necessarily linear.

The *FITT* framework identifies frequency, intensity, time and type. Frequency is how often activity occurs; intensity is how demanding it is; time is duration; type is the kind of activity. FITT is a planning framework, not a universal prescription or a substitute for individual assessment.

